s unit's explicit unchanged lightClusters-as-a-set contract. Restore an independent all-cluster comparison, and resolve the conservative-candidate mismatch rather than copying it into the expected result. Teardown judgment (note, not a proven new native-crash finding): scripts/gpu.fixture.ts:79-81 is the shared owner/disposal seam, and it still disposes worlds and the owning device synchronously without a drain. If disposing with pending queue work is the reproduced crash class, the lifetime fix belongs there, before world resources and then the device are released, with device-loss/validation failure settlement and cleanup preserved. cluster.gpu.test.ts:438-439 covers only this test's final successful path; exceptions skip it and other gpuApps files retain the same teardown shape. A single green CI run supports the local workaround but does not establish the crash's native root cause or remove the shared class. The test's comment also inaccurately attributes shared device release to the child app; gpuApps owns the device separately. Other prior items: the preceding-slice candidate fix is present and still bounded to at most 24 slices per light; broader scenes, populated-to-zero, overflow, distinct views, and aborted-draw retry assertions are present; commit evidence now reports count/populate mutations going red on both hosts. The projection acknowledgement fixes the originally reported unchanged-projection retry. Timestamp readback failure is now surfaced by profiler drain and the oracle injects a rejection. No performance judgment. Remaining uncertainty: did not independently rerun mutations, native shutdown reproduction, CI, device-loss/validation injections, snapshot/restore or device recreation. The local adapter reports no identity, so these independent reproductions are not a claimed Metal hardware qualification. No code edited.
